  "question": {
    "prompt": "\\(y=(2x+1)^2\\). What is the derivative at \\(x=1\\)?"
  },
  "schemaVersion": "3.0",
  "solutions": [
    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "cnm_h223",
        "cnm_h218"
      ],
      "explanation": "The outer derivative is \\(2(2x+1)\\), then multiply by the inner derivative \\(2\\). At \\(x=1\\), this is \\(2\\times3\\times2=12\\).",
      "id": "m1",
      "sourceRef": "cn-math/cnm_h223_q1#m1",
      "title": "Chain rule"
    },
    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "cnm_h222",
        "cnm_h218"
      ],
      "explanation": "Expanding the square turns the expression into a polynomial: \\(y=4x^2+4x+1\\). Differentiating term by term gives \\(y'=8x+4\\). Therefore \\(y'(1)=8+4=12\\).",
      "id": "m2",
      "sourceRef": "cn-math/cnm_h223_q1#m2",
      "title": "Expand before differentiating"
    }
  ],
  "sourceRef": "cn-math/cnm_h223_q1"
